-- title:
--   Recursion-tree unfolding of a decreasing specification
-- statement:
--   The unfolding of a decreasing-argument specification at input $n$ is the labelled ordered tree with root label $n$ and, in the specification's call order, the unfoldings at all immediate child inputs.
--
--   Decreasing child labels make this a finite tree. Repeated immediate calls produce repeated child subtrees.
-- source:
--   Swain, Recursive Call-Count Sequences: Structural Realisability, Periodic Branching, and Partial-Observation Reconstruction, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 79135884…), Section 5 and Theorem 5.5, pp.9–10

import Definitions.Def_CallCountPOPBR_Supplement_DecSpec
import Definitions.Def_CallCountPOPBR_Supplement_LTree

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace CallCountPOPBR.Supplement

/-- **The recursion tree generated by a specification** (§5, §5.2). Swain, *Recursive Call-Count Sequences: Structural Realisability, Periodic Branching, and
Partial-Observation Reconstruction*, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 79135884…), §5, p.9: "Let T_n be the
rooted recursion tree generated on input n"; §5.2, p.10, Theorem 5.5: "generated by a deterministic
decreasing-argument recursive specification".

`unfold s n` has root label `n` and, in order, one child subtree `unfold s k` for each immediate call
`R(k)` in `s.calls n`.

**Formalization Note.** A `DecSpec` is deterministic: `R(n)` always makes the same ordered list of
calls, so every call to `R(k)` unfolds to the same tree. -/
def DecSpec.unfold (s : DecSpec) (n : ℕ) : LTree :=
  .node n ((s.calls n).attach.map (fun k => s.unfold k.1))
termination_by n
decreasing_by exact s.dec n k.1 k.2

end CallCountPOPBR.Supplement


